    bio: "Shen Kuo was a Chinese polymath of the Song Dynasty whose range of intellectual achievement has rarely been equalled in any civilisation. Born into a family of civil servants in what is now Zhejiang province, he passed the imperial examinations and rose through the ranks of the bureaucracy while simultaneously pursuing investigations in astronomy, mathematics, geology, pharmacology, cartography, archaeology, and military strategy. He served as an ambassador to the Liao Dynasty in the north, directed hydraulic engineering works that reclaimed hundreds of thousands of acres of agricultural land, and reformed the Bureau of Astronomy, introducing more accurate measurement instruments and correcting centuries of accumulated error in the Chinese calendar.\n\nHis masterwork, the Mengxi Bitan — usually translated as Dream Pool Essays — is one of the most extraordinary documents in the history of science. Written during his retirement at his garden estate near Zhenjiang after falling out of political favour, it contains approximately six hundred essays on subjects ranging from the nature of fossils to the mathematics of musical scales to the mechanics of the tides. In it, Shen Kuo described the magnetic compass (one of the earliest detailed accounts of its use for navigation), provided the first known description of moveable type printing as invented by Bi Sheng, and offered a remarkably perceptive account of geological change — arguing from the evidence of fossilised shells found in mountain strata that the land had once been under the sea, centuries before European geology reached comparable conclusions.\n\nShen Kuo\'s observations on the nature of petroleum were equally prescient. Noticing black, oily seeps in Shaanxi province, he named the substance 'shiyou' — rock oil, the same term used in Chinese today — and predicted, with uncanny accuracy, that its supply was effectively inexhaustible and that it would one day be of great practical use to humanity. He also conducted careful astronomical observations that led him to propose that the pole star had shifted over time, implying a movement of the heavens that Chinese orthodoxy had not previously entertained. In cartography, he produced a large-scale relief map of the northern frontier that his superiors considered the most accurate ever made.\n\nWhat makes Shen Kuo remarkable is not merely the breadth of his curiosity but the rigour with which he pursued it. He was not a speculative philosopher but an empiricist who tested claims against observation, corrected received wisdom on the basis of evidence, and acknowledged his own uncertainty where it existed. Joseph Needham, the great historian of Chinese science, called him 'perhaps the most interesting character in all of Chinese scientific history.' He died in relative obscurity, his political enemies having overshadowed his reputation in his own lifetime — but the Dream Pool Essays survived, and through it, one of history\'s most remarkable minds remains perfectly legible to us, nine centuries on."
